  "text": "**Solution 2 is correct; Solution 1 is not.**\n\nThe statement is a quantitative Beurling–Helson theorem with a log-power threshold. Kahane conjectured that the sharp threshold is $c = 1$ (an $o(\\log|z|)$ bound forces affinity), but that conjecture is open. Solution 1's claim that Katznelson proved $c=1$ is therefore not right. The exponent proved in the literature is smaller.\n\nSolution 2 cites Konyagin and Shkredov's quantitative Beurling–Helson result, which gives the exponent $1/22$: if $\\|e^{-2\\pi i z\\phi}\\|_{A(\\mathbb{T})} = O(\\log^{\\gamma}|z|)$ with $\\gamma < 1/22$, then $\\phi$ is affine. I recall the $1/22$ from that paper's abstract, but I could not verify the citation here, so the identification of $c = 1/22$ rests on that recollection rather than a checked source.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
